    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Is this a good offer?\n\n**This is an exceptional, top-of-band offer for a Google L6 Product Manager in the Bay Area.** \n\nHere is a breakdown of how the numbers compare to both your current compensation and market benchmarks:\n\n#### Year 1 vs. Ongoing Total Compensation (TC)\n\n* **Base Salary:** **$260,000** (Near the ceiling of the posted $183k\u2013$271k band).\n* **Target Bonus (20%):** **$52,000**\n* **Sign-on Bonus:** **$200,000** (This completely offsets the $200,000 of Meta RSUs you would forfeit over the next 12 months).\n* **Equity:** **$1,340,000** over 4 years.\n  * Google typically utilizes a **front-loaded vesting schedule** (e.g., 33% / 33% / 22% / 12% or 38% / 32% / 20% / 10%).\n  * At a 33% Year 1 vest, your first-year equity alone is **~$442,200** (or **$335,000** on a flat 25% annualized schedule).\n\n| Metric | Current (Meta IC6) | Google L6 (Year 1) | Google L6 (4-Yr Avg, ex-sign-on) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base** | $240,000 | $260,000 | $260,000 |\n| **Bonus** | ~$36,000 | $52,000 | $52,000 |\n| **Stock Vesting** | $250,000 | ~$442,200 *(front-loaded)* | $335,000 |\n| **Sign-On** | \u2014 | $200,000 | \u2014 |\n| **Total Comp** | **$526,000** | **~$954,200** | **~$647,000/yr** |\n\n#### Market Context\n* **L6 Market Median:** Median Google L6 PM total compensation in the Bay Area generally sits around **$480,000\u2013$530,000/yr**.\n* **Your Offer:** Even without the sign-on bonus, your annualized average of **~$647,000/yr** puts you in the **90th+ percentile** for L6 PMs at Google.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Should you accept, negotiate, or walk away?\n\n* **Walk Away?** **No.** It is your top-choice company, an exciting team, and a significant step up in compensation.\n* **Accept outright vs. Negotiate?** **Negotiate once, lightly, using an \"agreement to sign\" lever.** \n\nBecause you do not have competing offers and Google has already given you a near-top-of-band offer, an aggressive push or bluffing about other opportunities can backfire. However, in Big Tech, making one polite, reasonable closing counter will not result in an offer being rescinded.\n\n---\n\n### 3. If you negotiate: What to ask for & exact phrasing\n\n#### What to ask for:\n1. **Equity (Primary Lever):** Ask to increase the 4-year grant from **$1.34M to $1.45M\u2013$1.5M** (~$360k\u2013$375k/year average). Recruiters have the most flexibility on equity bands.\n2. **Base Salary (Secondary Lever):** You are at $260,000 out of a $271,000 max. You can ask for **$270,000**, but equity will yield more upside.\n\n#### How to structure your request:\nUse an **\"Immediate Sign\" commitment**. Google recruiters love closing strong candidates if a modest adjustment guarantees an acceptance without further rounds of review.\n\n#### Suggested Script (via phone or email):\n\n> *\"Thank you so much for putting together such a strong offer. I've spent time reviewing it, and I'm genuinely thrilled about the team and the scope of this role\u2014Google remains my clear first choice.*\n>\n> *I want to make sure I am fully set up for the long term here. Given that Meta has historically maintained strong refresh cadence and my current base and equity trajectory are very solid, I would love to see if we can close the remaining gap on equity.* \n>\n> *If we can bring the initial equity grant to **$1,450,000** (or bring base to **$270,000**), I am ready to sign the offer immediately today.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n* If Google accepts the modest bump, you capture an extra ~$100k+ in equity over 4 years.\n* If Google says this is their final best package (which is possible given the strong initial numbers), you can graciously accept knowing you secured an upper-tier offer for L6 PM in the Bay Area.",
